---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Home Assistant + Tempo — Tempo"
  description="Send any Home Assistant state change, automation completion, or system event to Tempo and turn your HA dashboard from a snapshot into a timeline you can scroll back through."
>
  <header class="page-header">
    <nav class="page-nav">
      <a href="/docs">← Docs</a>
    </nav>
    <h1>Home Assistant</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Your dashboard tells you what is happening <em>now</em>. Tempo tells you what has happened <em>since</em>. Connect Home Assistant to Tempo with a single <code>rest_command</code> and every state change you care about — smoke detectors, leak sensors, doors, locks, alarm panels, automations completing — lands in a chronological feed on your Mac. No cloud round-trip, no separate query tool, just scroll-back history of your house.
    </p>
    <p class="lede-secondary">
      This page covers the five-minute setup, a copy-paste cookbook for the most common HA scenarios, and a short troubleshooting list. Tempo ships with a Home Assistant score (38 severity rules, 5 default actions, grouping by <code>entity_id</code>) so the events render with the right colour, label and buttons out of the box — you only have to send them.
    </p>
  </header>

  <main class="doc">

    <section class="scenario">
      <h2>Why connect HA to Tempo</h2>
      <p>
        Home Assistant is excellent at <em>state at a glance</em>. The Lovelace dashboard, the Energy panel, the History view — they all answer the question <em>what is the state of my house right now</em>. What they don't do well is the question <em>did the smoke detector trip last night, and what time, and was it before or after the dishwasher cycle finished</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Tempo is built for that second question. Every event you send arrives in a single chronological timeline on your Mac, grouped by entity, coloured by severity, ranked by recency. You can scroll back days, weeks, months. You can filter by source. You can click an event and jump straight to that entity's history page in HA. The two tools are complementary: HA is the brain that makes decisions, Tempo is the journal that remembers them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Tempo runs on your Mac, on your LAN, with no cloud component. The HA → Tempo link is a single HTTP POST inside your network — no third-party broker, no telemetry, no account.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="scenario">
      <h2>1. Five-minute setup</h2>
      <p>
        You will: (1) create an ingestion token in Tempo, (2) add a single
        <code>rest_command</code> to your HA configuration, (3) wire it into a
        test automation. Total time on a working setup: well under five minutes.
      </p>

      <h3>Create the token in Tempo</h3>
      <p>
        Open Tempo → <em>Settings</em> → <em>Database / Ingestion</em> →
        <em>Tokens</em>. Click <em>+ New token</em>, give it the name
        <code>home-assistant</code> (anything works, but matching the provider
        keeps the audit log readable), and bind it to provider
        <code>com.home-assistant</code>. Copy the token value — Tempo only
        shows it once at creation.
      </p>
      <p>
        Optional: click <em>Save in Keychain</em> on the same row. The token
        gets a Keychain entry tagged <em>Tempo: home-assistant</em> with iCloud
        Keychain sync, so you can recover it later from any of your Macs
        without rotating it.
      </p>

      <h3>Add the rest_command in HA</h3>
      <p>
        Edit your <code>configuration.yaml</code> (or a dedicated package) and
        add the block below. Replace <code>your-mac.local</code> with the
        actual hostname or LAN IP of the Mac running Tempo, and paste the
        token where indicated.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`rest_command:
  tempo_ingest:
    url: "http://your-mac.local:7776/ingest"
    method: POST
    headers:
      X-Tempo-Token: "PASTE_TOKEN_HERE"
      Content-Type: "application/json"
    payload: >
      {
        "title":              {{ ((trigger.to_state.attributes.friendly_name | default(trigger.entity_id)) ~ ' — ' ~ trigger.to_state.state) | tojson }},
        "providerIdentifier": "com.home-assistant",
        "eventType":          "alert",
        "externalID":         {{ trigger.entity_id | tojson }},
        "metadata": {
          "entity_id":     {{ trigger.entity_id | tojson }},
          "state":         {{ trigger.to_state.state | tojson }},
          "device_class":  {{ (trigger.to_state.attributes.device_class | default('')) | tojson }},
          "friendly_name": {{ (trigger.to_state.attributes.friendly_name | default(trigger.entity_id)) | tojson }},
          "area":          {{ (area_name(trigger.entity_id) | default('')) | tojson }},
          "last_changed":  {{ trigger.to_state.last_changed | string | tojson }}
        }
      }`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Reload <em>Developer Tools → YAML → All YAML configuration</em>, or
        restart HA. The <code>tempo_ingest</code> service is now available to
        every automation in the house.
      </p>

      <h3>Test from Developer Tools</h3>
      <p>
        Before wiring an automation, fire the service by hand to confirm the
        round-trip works. <em>Developer Tools → Services</em>, pick
        <code>rest_command.tempo_ingest</code>, and call it with no parameters
        from the UI — the template uses <code>trigger</code>, which is
        unbound here, so this manual call will fail safely. Instead, the
        easiest first test is the curl command below run from any host on
        your LAN:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`curl -X POST http://your-mac.local:7776/ingest \\
  -H "X-Tempo-Token: PASTE_TOKEN_HERE" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{
    "title": "HA test event",
    "providerIdentifier": "com.home-assistant",
    "eventType": "alert",
    "externalID": "test.tempo_setup",
    "metadata": {
      "entity_id": "test.tempo_setup",
      "state": "on",
      "friendly_name": "Tempo connectivity test",
      "device_class": "smoke"
    }
  }'`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        You should see the event appear in Tempo's timeline within a second,
        rendered as a critical "Smoke detected" event because the bundled
        score matches <code>device_class: smoke</code> + <code>state: on</code>.
        If nothing arrives, jump to <em>Troubleshooting</em> at the bottom of
        this page.
      </p>

      <h3>Wire your first real automation</h3>
      <p>
        Below is the smallest possible HA automation that sends a real state
        change to Tempo. It triggers on any change to a single entity and
        forwards the new state.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`alias: "Tempo: forward smoke detector"
trigger:
  - platform: state
    entity_id: binary_sensor.kitchen_smoke
action:
  - service: rest_command.tempo_ingest
mode: queued
max: 25`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Trigger it (press the test button on the detector, or change the
        entity state from Developer Tools), and you'll see two events in
        Tempo: one for the <code>on</code> transition, one for the
        <code>off</code> transition when the alarm clears. Because the
        bundled score groups by <code>entity_id</code>, the two events
        collapse into a single thread in the feed — easier to read than 38
        separate rows for a noisy sensor.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="scenario">
      <h2>2. Cookbook — common HA scenarios</h2>
      <p>
        These automations all use the <code>tempo_ingest</code>
        <code>rest_command</code> from section 1. Drop them into your
        automations file, change the <code>entity_id</code> values to match
        your house, and you're done.
      </p>

      <h3>Safety sensors (smoke, CO, leak, gas)</h3>
      <p>
        The bundled score automatically marks these as <em>critical</em> on
        <code>state: on</code> and as <em>ok</em> on <code>state: off</code>,
        so you don't need conditional logic — forward every state change and
        Tempo handles the colour and the label.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`alias: "Tempo: safety sensors"
trigger:
  - platform: state
    entity_id:
      - binary_sensor.kitchen_smoke
      - binary_sensor.basement_co
      - binary_sensor.laundry_leak
      - binary_sensor.boiler_gas
action:
  - service: rest_command.tempo_ingest
mode: queued
max: 25`}</code></pre>

      <h3>Doors, windows, locks</h3>
      <p>
        Pure state forwarding works the same way. The score recognises
        <code>binary_sensor.*_door*</code>,
        <code>binary_sensor.*_window*</code>, and <code>lock.*</code>
        patterns and renders them with neutral severity (informational,
        not alarm) so the feed doesn't scream every time you walk through
        the front door.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`alias: "Tempo: openings"
trigger:
  - platform: state
    entity_id:
      - binary_sensor.front_door
      - binary_sensor.bedroom_window
      - lock.front_door
action:
  - service: rest_command.tempo_ingest
mode: queued
max: 25`}</code></pre>

      <h3>Alarm panel</h3>
      <p>
        Same recipe — forward all states. The score recognises
        <code>alarm_control_panel.*</code> with states
        <code>triggered</code> (critical), <code>pending</code> (warning),
        <code>armed_away/home/night</code>, <code>arming</code>,
        <code>disarmed</code> (all info).
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`alias: "Tempo: alarm panel"
trigger:
  - platform: state
    entity_id: alarm_control_panel.house
action:
  - service: rest_command.tempo_ingest
mode: queued`}</code></pre>

      <h3>Climate and thresholds</h3>
      <p>
        For continuous values (temperature, humidity, energy), forwarding
        <em>every</em> state change would flood the feed. Use a numeric
        threshold trigger instead, so Tempo only sees the events you
        actually care about.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`alias: "Tempo: server room overheating"
trigger:
  - platform: numeric_state
    entity_id: sensor.server_room_temp
    above: 32
    for:
      minutes: 5
action:
  - service: rest_command.tempo_ingest
    data: {}
mode: single`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Note the <code>data: {}</code> — when the trigger isn't a state
        platform, <code>trigger.to_state</code> may not be available. For
        numeric triggers, replace the rest_command body with explicit
        values. Quick variant:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`# In configuration.yaml, alongside tempo_ingest:
rest_command:
  tempo_alert:
    url: "http://your-mac.local:7776/ingest"
    method: POST
    headers:
      X-Tempo-Token: "PASTE_TOKEN_HERE"
      Content-Type: "application/json"
    payload: >
      {
        "title":              {{ title | tojson }},
        "providerIdentifier": "com.home-assistant",
        "eventType":          "alert",
        "externalID":         {{ entity_id | tojson }},
        "metadata": {
          "entity_id":     {{ entity_id | tojson }},
          "state":         {{ state | tojson }},
          "friendly_name": {{ (friendly_name | default(entity_id)) | tojson }}
        }
      }`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Then call it from automations with explicit fields:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`action:
  - service: rest_command.tempo_alert
    data:
      title: "Server room hot: {{ states('sensor.server_room_temp') }}°C"
      entity_id: "sensor.server_room_temp"
      state: "warning"
      friendly_name: "Server room temperature"`}</code></pre>

      <h3>Person and presence</h3>
      <p>
        The score handles <code>person.*</code> and
        <code>device_tracker.*</code> with two states, <code>home</code> and
        <code>not_home</code>. Useful as an arrival/departure log.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`alias: "Tempo: presence"
trigger:
  - platform: state
    entity_id:
      - person.alex
      - person.sam
action:
  - service: rest_command.tempo_ingest
mode: queued`}</code></pre>

      <h3>Automation completed</h3>
      <p>
        Use this when you want a journal of <em>"the laundry-done routine
        ran at 22:14"</em> rather than a state change. Add the rest_command
        as the last action of any automation worth logging:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`alias: "Laundry done routine"
# ... your existing actions ...
action:
  # ... your real automation steps ...
  - service: rest_command.tempo_alert
    data:
      title: "Laundry cycle finished"
      entity_id: "automation.laundry_done"
      state: "info"
      friendly_name: "Laundry done routine"`}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="scenario">
      <h2>3. What the bundled score gives you</h2>
      <p>
        You don't have to design the rendering. The Home Assistant score
        ships with Tempo and provides:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>38 severity rules</strong> covering smoke, CO, gas, leak,
          tamper, alarm panels, doors, windows, locks, presence, motion,
          batteries, climate, automations, and HA system start/stop events.
          Each rule sets a severity colour and a short label
          (<em>Smoke</em>, <em>Cleared</em>, <em>Triggered</em>,
          <em>Armed away</em>, etc.) so the feed reads at a glance.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Grouping by <code>entity_id</code></strong> with a one-hour
          window. Repeated state flips on the same sensor collapse into one
          row in the feed; click to expand and see every transition with
          timestamps.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Five default action buttons</strong> on every HA event:
          <ul>
            <li><em>Open dashboard</em> — jumps to <code>http://homeassistant.local:8123</code></li>
            <li><em>Open entity history</em> — opens the HA history view filtered to that exact <code>entity_id</code></li>
            <li><em>Open automations</em> — opens the HA automation editor</li>
            <li><em>Copy entity ID</em> — clipboard, useful when writing a follow-up automation</li>
            <li><em>HA docs</em> — opens the Home Assistant documentation site</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If your HA instance is not at <code>homeassistant.local:8123</code>
        — for example you reach it at <code>http://192.168.1.50:8123</code>
        or via Nabu Casa Cloud — open Tempo's <em>Score Editor</em> and edit
        the URL fields once. The change persists across updates and applies
        to every HA event.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="scenario">
      <h2>4. Caveats and small print</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Always pipe through <code>tojson</code>.</strong> Every
          interpolated value in the payload above ends with
          <code>| tojson</code>. This is mandatory: HA's templating produces
          raw strings, and a sensor name with a quote, a backslash, or a
          control character will break the JSON without it. The
          <code>tojson</code> filter is the official escape hatch — use it
          on every value, even ones that "obviously can't contain quotes".
          They can.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Optional attributes need
          <code>default()</code>.</strong> Not every entity has
          <code>device_class</code>, <code>friendly_name</code>, or an area.
          Reading a missing attribute produces an undefined value that
          <code>tojson</code> renders as <code>null</code> — usually fine
          but occasionally surprising. Wrap optional reads in
          <code>(value | default('')) | tojson</code> so missing fields land
          as empty strings consistently.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>trigger.to_state</code> is a state object, not a
          string.</strong> Use <code>.state</code> for the value,
          <code>.attributes.X</code> for attributes,
          <code>.last_changed</code> for the timestamp. Do <em>not</em> use
          <code>states('entity_id')</code> inside a state-trigger automation
          — that returns the state at template-render time, which can race
          with the trigger and give you the previous value.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Numeric and event triggers don't have
          <code>trigger.to_state</code>.</strong> For these, use the
          <code>tempo_alert</code> variant from section 2 with explicit
          <code>data:</code> fields.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tempo must be reachable from HA on the LAN.</strong> The
          ingest server binds to all interfaces by default but listens on
          port 7776. If HA runs on a different machine (almost always the
          case), the Mac's firewall must allow inbound 7776, and any VLAN
          isolation must let HA reach the Mac. See the linked
          troubleshooting page below for the five most common network
          gotchas.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>One token per use case.</strong> If you also send Tempo
          events from a Pi-hole, an Uptime Kuma, or a script, use a
          separate token for each — the per-source audit log is then
          legible and revoking one source doesn't break the others.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="scenario">
      <h2>5. Troubleshooting</h2>
      <p>
        If the curl test in section 1 fails, the problem is almost certainly
        on the network — the Mac's firewall, Little Snitch, or a router
        rule. Tempo itself rarely fails silently at this stage. The
        dedicated page covers the five patterns that account for nearly
        every reported case:
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/scores/troubleshooting-networking"><strong>Troubleshooting networking →</strong></a>
      </p>
      <p>
        For HA-specific failures, check in this order:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>HA logs.</strong> <em>Settings → System → Logs</em> in HA.
          A failed <code>rest_command</code> shows as an error with the
          target URL and the response code. <code>401</code> means a wrong
          or missing token. <code>403</code> means the token is bound to a
          different provider, or the score channel is disabled in Tempo.
          <code>404</code> means the URL path is wrong (it must be exactly
          <code>/ingest</code>). Connection timeouts mean the Mac is not
          reachable.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Token in Tempo.</strong> Settings → Database / Ingestion →
          Tokens. Confirm the row exists and the binding shows
          <code>com.home-assistant</code>. If you regenerated the token via
          <em>Rotate</em>, the old value is invalid and must be replaced in
          HA.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Payload sanity.</strong> If events arrive but render with
          missing fields ("undefined" friendly name, no entity ID column,
          etc.), inspect the raw payload Tempo received. Open the event in
          Tempo, click <em>Inspect</em> (V1.1) or look at the audit log in
          <code>~/Library/Application Support/Tempo/</code>. The most
          common mistake is forgetting <code>| tojson</code> on a string
          field that contained a quote or a newline.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Score not applying.</strong> If events arrive but render
          flat (no severity colour, no "Smoke" label), verify the score is
          installed and enabled in Tempo's <em>Score Editor</em>. The
          providerIdentifier in your payload must be exactly
          <code>com.home-assistant</code>; any other value bypasses the
          score.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="scenario">
      <h2>6. What HA does not yet do well, and Tempo does</h2>
      <p>
        Two things, mainly. First, <strong>cross-source correlation</strong>:
        Tempo's feed mixes HA events with calendar entries, reminders,
        backup outcomes (Kopia), router events (UniFi), uptime checks
        (Kuma), CI builds (GitHub Actions), and anything else you forward
        — same screen, same severity scale, same scroll. When you're
        debugging a recurring 03:14 problem, seeing the HA leak alert
        adjacent to the failed Time Machine backup adjacent to the router
        reconnect <em>in a single column</em> shortens the
        diagnosis dramatically.
      </p>
      <p>
        Second, <strong>readable history without writing queries</strong>.
        HA's history view is excellent inside one entity. Across entities,
        across days, across types — it gets fiddly fast. Tempo is a
        chronological journal first; you scroll back, you see what
        happened, you don't write SQL.
      </p>
      <p>
        Tempo doesn't replace HA. It sits next to it on the Mac you
        already have open all day, and remembers the things HA does in the
        order they happened. That's it. If you've ever found yourself
        thinking <em>"I wish the dashboard had a notification log I could
        scroll back six hours"</em> — that's exactly what Tempo is.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="scenario footer-note">
      <h2>Next steps</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/scores">Browse other ready-made scores</a> — Kopia, UniFi, Uptime Kuma, GitHub Actions, Synology, and more</li>
        <li><a href="/scores/troubleshooting-networking">Troubleshooting networking</a> — when the curl test silently fails</li>
        <li><a href="/downloads">Download Tempo</a> — signed and notarised DMG, freeware</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

  </main>

